  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so there is zero information about which design choices (modality, profile, encoder, aggregation, fusion) matter. The single most important unknown is the performance floor of the simplest reasonable model. 2) What this tests: config_002 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000, no aggregation, no fusion) is the most standard and minimal RNA baseline in cellular profiling. It isolates the core task (classification from bulk/PCA-reduced RNA) without any learned encoders, multiprofile complexity, or fusion. 3) Why it is the best use of the first budget slot: Establishing this clean baseline is a prerequisite for interpreting every subsequent experiment—without it, we cannot determine whether multimodality, multiprofile aggregation, or learned encoders (scGPT, scVI) actually add value over a simple PCA projection. It is also fast to train (no heavy encoder), making it an efficient first data point. All future multimodal or multiprofile configurations can be directly compared against this floor to quantify the marginal contribution of each added design dimension."
